## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to translate product goals into a financially grounded technical plan, balancing on-device ML costs, user privacy, and rollout risk.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML cost modeling (energy, memory, inference latency)
- Data transfer savings and network impact
- Privacy controls and regulatory compliance
- OTA update cadence and maintenance costs
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost model for CBA calculations
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.inferences * params.energyPerInference;
  const memory = params.modelSize + params.cacheSize;
  const dataSavings = params.uploadSize * params.reductionRate;
  return { energy, memory, dataSavings };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a cost-aware, privacy-preserving on-device ML feature with hardware constraints and OTA workflows.

## Key Concepts
- On-device graph neural networks
- Privacy-preserving data handling
- OTA delta updates
- Drift detection and validation
- ECU hardware constraints

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code snippet for delta update application
function applyDelta(model, delta) {
  // merge weights with low-precision quantization
  for (let i=0; i<model.length; i++) model[i] = saturate(model[i] + delta[i], -128, 127);
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate privacy and security in OTA payload?
- How to handle drift when sensors drop or fail to report?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to formalize a cost-benefit for a privacy-preserving on-device biometric feature, balancing security, UX latency, and regulatory constraints.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML footprint and energy budgeting
- Privacy-by-design and regulatory alignment (DP, PCI-DSS)
- Drift detection, calibration, and validation cost modeling
- OTA bandwidth planning and rollout strategy

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch for enrollment and verification flow
function enroll(userAudio) {
  const embedding = model.extract(userAudio);
  secureStore.save(hash(embedding));
}
function verify(inputAudio) {
  const embedding = model.extract(inputAudio);
  return compare(hash(embedding), secureStore.getAllHashes());
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test spoofing resistance and liveness?
- What telemetry would you collect without compromising privacy?
- How would you handle template drift if a user changes voice over time?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model privacy-preserving analytics, not ML, with real-world trade-offs across storage, compute, energy, and compliance.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy budgets & CRDTs for local aggregation
- Edge-resource constraints and telemetry design
- Compliance costs and regulatory penalties

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple CRDT-like Grow-Only Counter
class GCounter { constructor() { this.counters = new Map(); }
inc(k, v=1){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0)+v,0)); }
merge(other){ for(const [k,v] of other.counters){ this.counters.set(k, Math.max((this.counters.get(k)||0), v)); } }
value(){ let s=0; for(const v of this.counters.values()) s+=v; return s; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle CRDT reconciliation when offline and merging after sync?
- What metrics would you track to detect drift in your aggregates and how would you price the analytics feature?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate a feature into a concrete CBA with privacy constraints, rollout strategy, and measurable ROI.

## Key Concepts

- On-device perceptual hashing
- Memory and CPU budgeting for fingerprint indices
- Data savings vs latency trade-offs
- Privacy/compliance, consent flows
- Rollout plans, offline fallback, drift maintenance, ROI calculation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: fingerprint a media item and decide upload
function shouldUpload(media) {
  const fp = perceptualFingerprint(media);
  const exists = fingerprintIndex.has(fp);
  if (exists) return false;
  fingerprintIndex.add(fp);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure and validate data savings at scale?
- How handle hash collisions and index eviction?
- What telemetry would you collect for ROI without compromising privacy?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess the ability to translate usage into a cost-benefit framework for a privacy-preserving, offline-first sync feature, including storage, energy, and cloud costs.

## Key Concepts

- Data sizing and retention policies
- On-device energy and encryption overhead
- Cloud storage/egress pricing and scaling
- Privacy/compliance considerations and opt-in flows
- Go/no-go criteria and break-even analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
function estimateAnnualCost({clipsPerDay, bytesPerClip, retentionDaysCloud, users, costPerGB}) {
  const perUserMB = clipsPerDay * bytesPerClip * 365 / (1024 * 1024);
  const cloudGB = clipsPerDay * bytesPerClip * retentionDaysCloud * 365 / (1024 * 1024 * 1024);
  const monthlyCost = (cloudGB * costPerGB) / 12;
  const totalMonthly = monthlyCost * users;
  return { perUserMB, cloudGB, totalMonthly };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust the CBA for user growth and volatility in clip frequency?
- What privacy/consent controls would you expose, and how would they impact costs and ROI?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to translate connectivity challenges into a minimal, privacy-conscious on-device flow with cost trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Edge batching, offline queues, deduping
- Energy/memory budgeting, latency impact
- Privacy, consent, rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
function batchRequests(requests, maxBatch) {
  const batches = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < requests.length; i += maxBatch) {
    batches.push(requests.slice(i, i + maxBatch));
  }
  return batches;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle failed batches in poor connectivity differently for iOS vs Android?
- What telemetry would you collect to validate break-even without compromising privacy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design privacy-preserving, device-local risk mechanisms for payments, balancing user friction, accuracy, and regulatory constraints.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML, secure enclave, latency budgeting
- Signal engineering for behavioral biometrics
- Regulatory/compliance (PCI, PSD2), consent and privacy
- Telemetry design (local only)
- Privacy risk management, false positives, rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo on-device inference
function score(signals, weights){
  let s=0;
  for(let i=0;i<weights.length;i++){
    s += signals[i]*weights[i];
  }
  return s;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure latency and energy per payment?
- How would you test false positives and user friction trade-offs?
- What governance would you implement for code and data in the secure enclave?

## Why This Is Asked
CBA for an offline-first backup feature requires tangible cost knobs and measurable outcomes beyond accuracy or latency; beginner-friendly but demonstrates ability to quantify trade-offs and plan rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Energy budgeting per photo and dedup impact
- Data minimization and privacy/compliance
- Rollout, offline fallback, drift/maintenance, break-even

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo energy check before upload
function shouldUpload(photoSizeMB, currentEnergyJ, thresholdJ){
  const dedupFactor = 1.5;
  const energyPerMB = 0.25;
  const energy = photoSizeMB * energyPerMB * dedupFactor;
  return (currentEnergyJ - energy) > thresholdJ;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure de-dup effectiveness in production?
- What metrics trigger rollback during rollout?
